Strip the CR of CRLF lines in makefiles on Windows too.

GNU make leaves that to the C runtime's text mode on Windows.  Linked
with posix-env's binmode.o, make reads makefiles in binary mode, so a
CRLF line kept its CR and "\" before CRLF was no continuation.  gcc
writes its -MF dependency files with CRLF, so automake's dependency
style check failed and builds fell back to depmode=none (no header
dependencies).

--- a/src/read.c
+++ b/src/read.c
@@ -2699,9 +2699,10 @@
       /* We got a newline, so add one to the count of lines.  */
       ++nlines;
 
-#if !defined(WINDOWS32) && !defined(__MSDOS__) && !defined(__EMX__)
+#if !defined(__MSDOS__) && !defined(__EMX__)
       /* Check to see if the line was really ended with CRLF; if so ignore
-         the CR.  */
+         the CR.  On Windows too: makefiles are read in binary mode when
+         make is linked that way.  */
       if ((p - start) > 1 && p[-2] == '\r')
         {
           --p;
